^TBD(260,"K104.018774")="e.g. `Label @en`"
^TBD(260,"K104.018790")="e.g. a name of `claudio_arrau` yields `sandbox.obj.claudio_arrau`, `sandbox.person.claudio_arrau`, `sandbox.pianist.claudio_arrau` and so on. @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.018797")="e.g. (lat -36.60664, lng -72.10344) @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.0187A3")="e.g. An example must always start with the prefix `e.g` @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.0187D3")="e.g. sys.attr.cardinality has range sys.val.ref and expected type sys.enum.cardinality, meaning its values must reference an instance of sys.enum.cardinality @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.0187E7")="e.g. sys.enum.cardinality groups sys.enum.cardinality.one and sys.enum.cardinality.many as its closed set of constants @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.0187ED")="e.g. sys.enum.cardinality.one restricts an attribute to a single value; sys.enum.cardinality.many allows multiple values @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.018801")="e.g. sys.enum.unique.insert enforces uniqueness on insert only; sys.enum.unique.upsert unifies incoming data with an existing entity on match @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.018815")="e.g. sys.cvt.height groups a magnitude field and a unit field as the fixed set of roles composing a height value @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.01881D")="e.g. latitude=-36.60664, longitude=-72.10344, altitude=140 @en"
^TBD(260,"K104.018831")="e.g. an object to describe actor Tom Hanks. Objects in HermesBase are used to represent a wide range of topics, including people, places, events, books, movies, and more. Object is the specific instance of any topic within HermesBase. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186A6")="A meta-level concept type whose instances denote atomic data types, whose values form the range of attributes. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186AC")="Atomic Value Type representing a reference to an object. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186B3")="Atomic Value Type representing a unique, human-readable identifier for objects. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186B9")="Atomic Value Type representing fixed long signed integers (64bits wide). @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186BF")="Atomic Value Type representing textual data @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186C5")="Atomic Value Type representing Boolean 0:False, 100:True @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186CB")="Atomic Value Type for Unix timestamps. Stored internally as the 0x number of microseconds since January 1, 1970 00:00:00 UTC @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186D1")="Atomic Value Type for UUID4 @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186D7")="Atomic Value Type representing a double-precision (64-bit) floating point number @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186DD")="Atomic Value Type for URLs @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186E3")="Atomic Value Type that stores the short human-readable identifier of an entity @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186E9")="Atomic Value Type that represents a date with year, month, day in ISO-8601 extended format @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186EF")="Atomic Value Type that represents a time with hour, minute, and second in ISO-8601 format @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186F5")="Atomic Value Type for datetime in ISO-8601 format @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0186FB")="Atomic Value Type that represents a geographic point encoded as an OGC Well-Known Text (WKT) POINT geometry with longitude and latitude coordinates @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018701")="Atomic Value Type that represents a geographic point encoded as an OGC Well-Known Text (WKT) POINT Z geometry with longitude, latitude, and altitude coordinates @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018706")="Atomic Value Type that represents alternative, easily memorable, user-friendly references to the same entity @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01870D")="Atomic Value Type that stores a MUMPS extrinsic function name, distinguished by the mandatory $$Tag^Routine syntax @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018714")="Atomic Value Type that stores a language-tagged string, distinguished from a plain string by the mandatory @<lang> suffix @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01871B")="Atomic Value Type that stores a geohash encoding of a geographic position derived from latitude and longitude coordinates. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018722")="Atomic Value Type that stores a GPS geographic coordinate pair represented as latitude and longitude decimal degrees according to the WGS 84 coordinate reference system @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018728")="A meta-level concept type whose instances denote properties or characteristics that can be assigned to objects. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018730")="Atomic multi-value attribute that triggers enforcement of a named entity spec against the asserting entity @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018739")="Atomic multi-value attribute that specifies the set of attributes that must be present in an entity, enforced by sys.attr.entspec @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018741")="Atomic multi-value attribute that specifies a list of fully-qualified predicates invoked by sys.attr.entspec for entity validation @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018747")="Atomic multi-value attribute that defines validation predicates applied to an attribute's value when it is asserted @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01874F")="Atomic multi-value attribute that stores a short abbreviation for an entity @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018758")="Atomic single-value attribute that assigns a unique, human-readable identifier to an object in its namespace @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018760")="Atomic single-value attribute that defines the permitted value type for another attribute @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018767")="Atomic single-value attribute that specifies whether another attribute is mandatory or not @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01876E")="Atomic multi-value attribute that provides guidance when and why to use a specific entity @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018775")="Atomic multi-value attribute that provides the canonical human-readable display name for the entity, potentially translated in other languages @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01877A")="Enumerated single-value attribute that defines whether another attribute holds one or many values @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018782")="Enumerated single-value attribute that defines the uniqueness constraint behavior applied to another attribute, determining whether values are inserted as unique entries or used to unify with existing entities based on identity matching. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018789")="Atomic single-value attribute that defines whether another attribute is indexed. If true, attribute values are added to AVET index @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018791")="Atomic single-value attribute that is the short human-readable identifier for the entity, forming the last segment of its dot-notated key. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018798")="Atomic multi-value attribute that provides a generic, human-readable friendly display value for the entity @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01879D")="Atomic multi-value attribute that assigns one or more alternative names to an object, enabling user-friendly, searchable, and easily memorable references to the same entity. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187A4")="Atomic multi-value attribute that stores one or more representative examples illustrating the typical use or expected values of the entity. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187A9")="Atomic multi-value attribute that stores a human-readable description of an object, potentially translated in other languages @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187B3")="The URL of a Wikipedia page that is related to an object @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187B9")="The official website (homepage) of an object @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187BE")="Atomic multi-value attribute that assigns an external identifier, vocabulary code to an object, linking it to a corresponding entity in an external database system. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187C5")="Atomic single-value attribute that identifies an existing entity only by a persisted EID generated by GENID. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187CC")="Atomic single-value attribute that identifies an entity by a unique-attribute that resolves to an existing EID. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187D4")="Atomic multi-value attribute that constrains a reference-typed attribute by specifying expected entity types of the referenced value. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187DA")="Atomic multi-value attribute that allow storing multiple note values, each language-tagged. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187E1")="Atomic single-value attribute that declares the type that this entity is an instance of @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187E8")="A meta-level concept type whose instances denote enumerated value types, each grouping a closed set of named constants used as the range of constrained attributes. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187EE")="Enumerated value type representing the set of cardinality constraints used by `sys.attr.cardinality`. Valid instances are sys.enum.cardinality.one and sys.enum.cardinality.many. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187F4")="A value representing a cardinality constraint used by `sys.attr.cardinality` to restrict an attribute to exactly one value. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.0187FB")="A value representing a cardinality constraint used by `sys.attr.cardinality` to allow an attribute to hold multiple values. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018802")="Enumerated value type representing the set of uniqueness constraint used by `sys.attr.unique`. Valid instances are sys.enum.unique.insert and sys.enum.unique.upsert. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018807")="A value representing a uniqueness constraint used by `sys.attr.unique` that enforces uniqueness of attribute values across entities, preventing duplicates during entity creation. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01880E")="A value representing a uniqueness constraint used by `sys.attr.unique` that enables identity-based uniqueness, implementing upsert semantics (insert or update depending on existence). @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018816")="A meta-level concept type whose instances denote composite value types, where each grouping represents a single structured value, similar to a Freebase Compound Value Type. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01881E")="Composite Value Type representing a geographic location composed of coordinate fields such as latitude, longitude, and optional elevation metadata. Instances of this entity type represent geographic positions. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018825")="A meta-level concept type whose instances denote foundational entity types in HermesBase, forming the cornerstone of HermesBase type system. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.01882B")="A meta-level concept type whose instances denote relationship types that collectively describe objects, analogous to relations in the Relational Model. @en"
^TBD(262,"K106.018832")="A meta-level concept type whose instances are objects. Each object is back-linked with one or more `associations` that collectively describe the object from various perspectives @en"
